    "https://ulsuno.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UniversityOfNewOrleans/job/New-Orleans-La/Assistant-Professor-of-Education_R-002213-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nThank you for your interest in LSU New Orleans. Once you start the application process, you will not be able to save your work, so you should collect all required information before you begin.  The required information is listed below in the job posting.   You must complete all required portions of the application and attach the required documents in order to be considered for employment.   Department Curriculum, Instruction and Special Education Job Summary Job Description The School of Education at Louisiana State University – New Orleans invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Teacher Education with an expertise in elementary grades subject matter and/or pedagogy The successful candidate will have an excellent track record of K12 teaching, a preference for collaborative work, a strong commitment to novice teacher development, and an established or emerging research agenda in an area of teacher education. This position is responsible for 9 academic units per semester (3/3). Responsibilities include teaching graduate and undergraduate courses in teacher education, maintaining productive scholarly activity in an area of K12 education, and service to the campus, community, and the profession. Other responsibilities may include supervision of Resident Teachers in their school placements, curriculum and institutional effectiveness management (with the Program Coordinator), recruitment, community outreach, inclusion on university committees, and other support of the administrative functions of the unit. How to Apply  Please submit: (1) a curriculum vitae (2) a letter of interest in the position, noting achievements in teaching and scholarship (3) An example of your scholarly work (4) a one-page teaching philosophy, and (5) the names and contact information for three references. Application materials should be submitted via the University's application portal, attn: Director of the School of Education, Dr. Brian Beabout, by December 1, 2026 . While we will begin reviewing and scheduling interviews at that time, the position will remain open until filled, and applications will be considered after the deadline if a preferred candidate is not identified in the first round of submissions. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. or other terminal degree in Teacher Education, Curriculum & Instruction, or a related field. Evidence of successful K12 teaching experience. An active research agenda in an area of K12 education. Preferred Qualifications    Evidence of teaching excellence at the University level Demonstrable expertise in teaching students from diverse backgrounds and with diverse academic and developmental needs Applications that do not include the required uploaded documents may not be considered. Posting Close Date This position will remain open until filled. Note to Applicant: Applicants should fully describe their qualifications and experience with specific reference to each of the minimum and preferred qualifications in their cover letter. The search committee will use this information during the initial review of application materials.    References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process.    This position may require a criminal background check to be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire.    As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions at LSU New Orleans may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. LSU New Orleans seeks to recruit a highly productive workforce and will provide equal employment opportunities to all employees and prospective employees. Employment decisions shall be based strictly on merit and without regard to religious or political beliefs, sex, race, or any other non-merit factor."
    },
    "https://ulsuno.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UniversityOfNewOrleans/job/New-Orleans-La/Assistant-Professor---Professional-Practice--Contemporary---Commercial-Music--Jazz--and-Music-Production_R-002201": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Professional Practice, Contemporary & Commercial Music, Jazz, and Music Production",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nThank you for your interest in LSU New Orleans. Once you start the application process, you will not be able to save your work, so you should collect all required information before you begin.  The required information is listed below in the job posting.   You must complete all required portions of the application and attach the required documents in order to be considered for employment.   Department Music Job Summary Job Description Job Description The School of the Arts/Music Program at LSU New Orleans invites applications for a full-time Assistant Professor, Professional Practice in Music position with expertise in contemporary and commercial music, and music production. The successful candidate will be a versatile musician, educator, and creative practitioner whose work is rooted in the foundations of American music, with a strong understanding of jazz and Black American musical traditions and their continued influence on contemporary music. Expertise in performance, songwriting, film scoring, composition, arranging, recording and audio engineering. Music technology, music business, and/or contemporary performance practice is highly desirable. The successful candidate will be an inspiring individual dedicated to mentorship, with the ability to connect historical and cultural musical foundations to the creative, technological, and professional realities of today’s evolving music industry. The Music Program seeks a forward-thinking educator capable of contributing across multiple areas of music instruction, supporting the broader contemporary and commercial music pathways. The ideal candidate will demonstrate artistic versatility, technical fluency, and a commitment to developing students as innovative musicians, collaborative artists, and creative professionals. Responsibilities The position will include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses according to the needs of the Music Program and the candidate’s areas of expertise. Teaching responsibilities may include improvisation, music history, film scoring, songwriting, theory, arranging, music production, recording and audio engineering, music technology, music business, music history, and other contemporary music subjects. Additional responsibilities include recruitment, course creation, audition reviews, community outreach, participation in departmental performances and events, collaboration with faculty across the Music Program and School of the Arts, and support of the administrative functions of the unit. The successful candidate is expected to maintain an active record of professional, scholarly, or creative activity appropriate to the position. How to Apply Application materials should be submitted through the university’s official application portal. A complete application should include: 1.        A curriculum vitae. 2.        A letter of interest outlining qualifications, relevant professional experience, and teaching accomplishments. 3.        Samples of creative or professional work, which may include recordings, compositions, arrangements, productions, or other relevant projects. 4.        A one-page teaching philosophy. 5.        Names and contact information for three professional references. Review Date Review of applications will begin on April 15, 2027. For full consideration your complete application should be submitted via the application portal by 11:59 PM on April 14, 2027 . The position will remain open until filled, and applications will be considered after the deadline if a preferred candidate is not identified in the first round of submissions. Required Qualifications A minimum master’s degree in Music, Contemporary or Commercial Music, Jazz Studies, Music Production, Composition, or a closely related field. Demonstrated professional and/or academic expertise in contemporary and commercial music, jazz, music production, or related creative disciplines. Evidence of successful teaching, mentorship, or equivalent professional educational experience. Demonstrated ability to teach and contribute across multiple areas of a university music curriculum. Working knowledge of contemporary music technology and its application in creative and educational environments. Demonstrated understanding of jazz, American music, and the historical and cultural foundations of Black American musical traditions. Preferred Qualifications A Ph.D., DMA, or other relevant doctoral degree. Demonstrated expertise in multiple areas of contemporary music practice, including songwriting, arranging, commercial composition, music production, recording engineering, music business, film and media scoring, and/or music technology. Professional experience with digital audio workstations, recording systems, audio production, editing, signal flow, and contemporary studio practices. Experience teaching music history, improvisation, harmony, or related courses. Evidence of professional recognition through performance, recording, composition, production, songwriting, publishing, or other creative work. Experience in curriculum development, particularly in contemporary music, commercial music, songwriting, or music production. A successful history of entrepreneurship, interdisciplinary collaboration, fundraising, grant writing, community engagement, or creative industry partnerships. A demonstrated ability to recruit, mentor, and develop students from diverse musical backgrounds and artistic interests. Salary Information Commensurate with qualifications and experience. This position is subject to university and budgetary approval. Music at LSU New Orleans The Music Program at LSU New Orleans is rooted in the rich musical and cultural heritage of New Orleans, a city foundational to the development of jazz and American popular music. Building upon this legacy, the Music Program continues to evolve toward a broader educational model that connects the historical foundations of jazz and Black American music with contemporary and commercial music, songwriting, composition, recording, production, technology, and the music industry. The program seeks to cultivate artists who understand the traditions from which American music emerged while developing the creative identities, technical abilities, collaborative skills, and professional knowledge necessary to shape its future. The successful candidate will play an important role in advancing this mission, supporting existing areas of study while helping strengthen and expand the Music Program’s contemporary curriculum. Applications that do not include the required uploaded documents may not be considered. Posting Close Date This position will remain open until filled. Note to Applicant: Applicants should fully describe their qualifications and experience with specific reference to each of the minimum and preferred qualifications in their cover letter. The search committee will use this information during the initial review of application materials.    References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process.    This position may require a criminal background check to be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire.    As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions at LSU New Orleans may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. LSU New Orleans seeks to recruit a highly productive workforce and will provide equal employment opportunities to all employees and prospective employees. Employment decisions shall be based strictly on merit and without regard to religious or political beliefs, sex, race, or any other non-merit factor."
    }
